\begin{abstract}
\michaelreplace{Large-scale epidemic forecasts are often made under incomplete contact-network information. A fully microscopic model may define the reference spreading process, but the modeller may only know local community structure, aggregate mobility weights, or community-level prevalence. We study this situation for two-scale stochastic epidemic dynamics on community networks. The full-information reference is a continuous-time Markov chain (CTMC) on a contact graph with a fixed community partition. The incomplete-information model preserves exact local CTMC dynamics inside communities, but replaces cross-community edges by macro-level import hazards and placement rules based on retained summaries. The central question is not computational speedup, but predictive information loss: what is lost when the full contact network is replaced by a micro--macro abstraction, and how does the loss affect inter-community first-arrival predictions? We make four contributions. First, we formulate two-scale epidemic modelling as conditional prediction under nested information sets. Second, we prove a value-of-information identity showing that enriching the interface can only reduce mean-squared prediction error for the full inter-community intensity, with the improvement equal to a conditional-variance reduction. Third, we show that community prevalence is not generally sufficient: microscopic states with identical community counts can have different active cross-community exposure and different edge-conditioned target laws. Fourth, we analyze two benchmark families. The clique--bridge--leaf motif gives a minimal non-identifiability example and shows, via a Jensen survival gap, why a mean hazard need not preserve first-arrival distributions. Chains of cliques then show how local interface discrepancies can accumulate along a community path. The paper identifies interface exposure and target placement as key information variables for faithful two-scale epidemic prediction, and provides a theoretical foundation for later empirical validation on real contact networks.}
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} applied network science; epidemic networks; incomplete information; two-scale dynamics; community networks; stochastic spreading; continuous-time Markov chains; first-arrival prediction; network abstraction

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

\michaelreplace{Epidemic spreading on large networks is shaped by mechanisms observed at different resolutions. Within a local community, such as a school, workplace, city district, or household cluster, infection and recovery occur as stochastic person-to-person events on a contact network. Between communities, infection is seeded by commuting, travel, shared institutions, or other inter-community contacts. A fully microscopic model would specify all of these contacts simultaneously. In practice, however, this full contact network is rarely known. Some local contact structure may be observed or generated synthetically, whereas inter-community coupling is often available only through aggregate mobility, coarse flow estimates, or weighted links between communities.}

\michaelreplace{This paper studies two-scale epidemic dynamics as prediction under incomplete network information. The full contact network is treated as the reference object: if every edge and every node state were known, it would determine the exact CTMC intensity of each possible transmission. The two-scale abstraction observes less. It may keep exact stochastic dynamics inside each community, but communicate between communities only through aggregate summaries such as prevalence, susceptibility, and coupling weights. The scientific question is therefore not whether a reduced model is faster. It is what prediction accuracy is lost when contact information is hidden, and which additional interface summaries are most valuable for recovering it.}

Network epidemic theory has established that contact heterogeneity and network structure influence epidemic thresholds, invasion, extinction, and timing \citep{keeling2005networks,pastor2015epidemic}. Metapopulation and mobility-network models provide scalable representations of spatial spread by coupling aggregate populations through flows \citep{colizza2008epidemic,balcan2009multiscale,ajelli2010comparing}. Exact stochastic simulation algorithms, including Gillespie-type methods and network or temporal variants, provide a continuous-time reference for event-level epidemic dynamics \citep{gillespie1977exact,anderson2007modified,vestergaard2015temporal,kuryliak2025gillespie}. A related reduction literature studies lumping, Markov population abstractions, and structural coarse graining of spreading processes on networks \citep{gfeller2007spectral,grossmann2019reducing,ward2022micro}. Applied Network Science explicitly encourages applied research using network science, including theory with clear applied impact \citep{menezes2016editorial}; this motivates a contribution that is theoretical in form but targeted at practical epidemic forecasting with incomplete network data.

\michaelreplace{The gap is that existing multiscale and coarse-grained epidemic models often specify an aggregate interface without isolating the information value of the omitted microscopic contacts. If a community exports infection through a small set of bridge nodes, then overall prevalence may be a poor proxy for export readiness. If an imported infection is placed uniformly in the destination community, then the post-import local outbreak may differ from the full contact process in which the target is selected through a specific active edge. These issues are not implementation details. They are information constraints: the macro layer cannot condition on facts that it does not observe.}

\michaelreplace{The paper contributes a theory of what the micro--macro abstraction loses relative to the full contact network. The first contribution is an explicit full-information CTMC reference and an incomplete-information two-scale model. The second contribution is a value-of-information theorem for inter-community intensity prediction under nested information sets. The third contribution is a set of exactness and non-identifiability results: local CTMC exactness does not imply full-process exactness; prevalence-only hazards are not exact unless the active interface exposure is determined by the retained macro variables; and exact target placement requires the edge-conditioned target distribution. The fourth contribution is a pair of network benchmark families that make the theory visible. The clique--bridge--leaf motif isolates one interface, while a chain of cliques repeats the interface to show how local prediction differences can accumulate over a network path.}

\michaelreplace{No real-world case study is included in this theory draft by design. Real contact datasets introduce confounders that are orthogonal to the present question: uncertain community partitions, unobserved contacts, temporal sampling, privacy-preserving aggregation, behavior change, and unknown calibration of transmission rates. A field case is essential for the next empirical step, but it is not the cleanest way to prove which information is lost by abstraction. Here we use controlled network motifs because they provide a known full-information reference and allow causal statements about the value of specific interface summaries. The intended sequence is therefore theory first, empirical validation second.}

The paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:related} positions the work relative to network epidemiology, stochastic simulation, metapopulation modelling, and network reduction. Section~\ref{sec:information_view} introduces the full-information versus incomplete-information perspective. Section~\ref{sec:reference} defines the full CTMC. Section~\ref{sec:twoscale} defines the two-scale abstraction. Section~\ref{sec:value_information} gives the theoretical results. Sections~\ref{sec:clique_leaf} and~\ref{sec:chain_cliques} develop the clique--bridge--leaf and chain-of-cliques benchmark families. Section~\ref{sec:scope} explains the scope and the role of future real-world validation. Section~\ref{sec:discussion} concludes.

