\section{Related work, scope, and novelty}
\label{sec:related}

\subsection{Network epidemics and stochastic event timing}

Network epidemic models represent spreading as a process on nodes and edges. This perspective is essential when degree heterogeneity, community structure, bridges, or temporal contacts influence invasion and extinction \citep{keeling2005networks,pastor2015epidemic}. The exact CTMC or Gillespie representation is a natural reference for such processes because event times are generated from current propensities rather than from a fixed time grid \citep{gillespie1977exact}. Extensions such as modified next-reaction methods and temporal Gillespie algorithms clarify how time-dependent propensities and changing contact structure can be handled by integrated hazards \citep{anderson2007modified,vestergaard2015temporal}.

\subsection{Metapopulation models and two-scale abstractions}

Metapopulation epidemic models replace detailed contacts by aggregate subpopulations connected through mobility or transportation patterns \citep{colizza2008epidemic,balcan2009multiscale,ajelli2010comparing}. They are powerful when the main question concerns large-scale spatial spread and when detailed contact data are unavailable. The present work is close in spirit but asks a more specific network-science question: if local dynamics inside communities remain microscopic and stochastic, what must the inter-community interface know to preserve first-arrival predictions? In this sense, the two-scale model is not only a computational device. It is also an information model.

\subsection{Network coarse graining and reduction}

A broad literature studies graph coarse graining, lumpability, aggregation of Markov processes, and reductions of dynamics on networks \citep{gfeller2007spectral,grossmann2019reducing,ward2022micro}. These methods typically ask how a large network process can be represented by fewer states, fewer nodes, or a lower-dimensional population model. Our novelty is complementary: we do not aggregate the local CTMC inside each community. Instead, we hold local stochastic dynamics fixed and analyze the information bottleneck at the inter-community interface. This leads to different objects of interest: active cross-community exposure, edge-conditioned target placement, and first-arrival timing.

\subsection{Novelty in one paragraph}

\michaelreplace{The main novelty is the separation of local stochastic exactness from interface information. A two-scale model can simulate every community exactly between imports and still produce different inter-community predictions if the macro interface cannot see the active transmission edges. We formalize this as conditional prediction under incomplete information, prove a value-of-information identity for inter-community intensities, show non-identifiability of prevalence-only hazards, provide exactness conditions for a sufficient interface, and use transparent network motifs to visualize why the effect appears. This makes the paper suitable for Applied Network Science: it studies dynamics on and through networks, but its contribution is a network-science theory of which structural information is needed for faithful prediction.}

