This framing separates the algorithmic question from the informational question. The algorithmic question asks how quickly a specified stochastic process can be simulated. The informational question asks how accurately the full process can be predicted from the summaries retained by an abstraction. This paper focuses on the informational question.

\section{Full-information reference process}
\label{sec:reference}

Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite directed graph of microscopic transmission channels. An undirected contact can be represented by two directed channels. The node set is partitioned into $M$ communities,
\begin{equation}
  V=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{M}V_i,\qquad N_i=|V_i|.
\end{equation}
For $i\neq j$, define the oriented cross-community edge set
\begin{equation}
  E_{ij}=\{(u,v)\in E:u\in V_i,\ v\in V_j\},
\end{equation}
and let $E_{ii}$ denote within-community edges. At time $t$, node $v$ has state $X_v(t)\in\{S,I,R\}$. In community $i$ we write
\begin{align*}
S_i(t)&=\{v\in V_i:X_v(t)=S\},& s_i(t)&=|S_i(t)|,& \bar s_i(t)&=s_i(t)/N_i,\\
I_i(t)&=\{v\in V_i:X_v(t)=I\},& i_i(t)&=|I_i(t)|,& \bar i_i(t)&=i_i(t)/N_i,\\
R_i(t)&=\{v\in V_i:X_v(t)=R\},& r_i(t)&=|R_i(t)|,& \bar r_i(t)&=r_i(t)/N_i.
\end{align*}
Capital letters denote node sets, lower-case letters denote counts, and barred quantities denote fractions.

For a configuration $x\in\{S,I,R\}^{V}$, infection along an oriented edge $(u,v)\in E$ has propensity
\begin{equation}
  a_{u\to v}(x)=\beta_{uv}\ind\{x_u=I,\ x_v=S\},
\end{equation}
and recovery of node $u$ has propensity
\begin{equation}
  a_u^{\mathrm{rec}}(x)=\gamma_u\ind\{x_u=I\}.
\end{equation}
Let $x^{v:I}$ be the configuration obtained by changing $v$ to state $I$, and let $x^{u:R}$ be the configuration obtained by changing $u$ to state $R$. The full-network CTMC has generator
\begin{align}
(\calL f)(x)
&=\sum_{(u,v)\in E}\beta_{uv}\ind\{x_u=I,\ x_v=S\}\,[f(x^{v:I})-f(x)] \notag\\
&\quad +\sum_{u\in V}\gamma_u\ind\{x_u=I\}\,[f(x^{u:R})-f(x)]
\label{eq:full_generator}
\end{align}
for bounded test functions $f$. This full process is the reference model.

The exact instantaneous cross-community infection intensity from $i$ to $j$ at state $x$ is
\begin{equation}
  a^\star_{ij}(x)=\sum_{(u,v)\in E_{ij}}\beta_{uv}\ind\{x_u=I,\ x_v=S\}.
  \label{eq:exact_cross_intensity}
\end{equation}
The associated full-information target distribution, conditional on an $i\to j$ infection event, is
\begin{equation}
  \pi^\star_{ij}(v\mid x)
  =\frac{\ind\{x_v=S\}\sum_{u\in V_i:(u,v)\in E_{ij}}\beta_{uv}\ind\{x_u=I\}}
  {a^\star_{ij}(x)},\qquad v\in V_j,
  \label{eq:exact_target_distribution}
\end{equation}
whenever $a^\star_{ij}(x)>0$. Equation~\eqref{eq:exact_cross_intensity} is the rate information and Eq.~\eqref{eq:exact_target_distribution} is the placement information that may be lost under abstraction.

\section{Two-scale model under incomplete network information}
\label{sec:twoscale}

The two-scale model preserves exact CTMC dynamics on each induced local graph $G_i=(V_i,E_{ii})$. Between external imports, each community evolves by the same infection and recovery rules as the full CTMC restricted to $V_i$. Inter-community transmission is represented by a macro import process on the directed graph of communities.

A common incomplete-information macro hazard is
\begin{equation}
  \lambda_{ij}(t)=\beta_{\mathrm{out}}W_{ij}\bar i_i(t)\bar s_j(t),
  \label{eq:prevalence_macro_hazard}
\end{equation}
where $W_{ij}\geq0$ is a directed community-coupling weight. In an empirical setting, $W_{ij}$ may be a mobility volume or estimated interaction strength. In a controlled benchmark, it may be chosen as the number or total weight of cross-community contacts. Unless additional assumptions make community prevalence representative of cross-community endpoints, Eq.~\eqref{eq:prevalence_macro_hazard} is a predictor of Eq.~\eqref{eq:exact_cross_intensity}, not an identity.

The total macro hazard is
\begin{equation}
  \Theta(t)=\sum_{i\neq j}\lambda_{ij}(t).
\end{equation}
Conditional on the aggregate path, macro event timing is generated by the cumulative-hazard equation
\begin{equation}
  \int_{t_0}^{t^*}\Theta(s)\,ds=E,
  \qquad E\sim\Exp(1).
  \label{eq:macro_budget}
\end{equation}
When a macro event occurs and $\Theta(t)>0$, the pair $i\to j$ is selected with probability $\lambda_{ij}(t)/\Theta(t)$. A destination node is then selected from $S_j(t)$ according to an import-placement rule $\Pi_{ij}(\cdot\mid t)$. Uniform placement is simple, but it is generally not equivalent to the full-information distribution in Eq.~\eqref{eq:exact_target_distribution}.

