\subsection{Placement information}

Rate information determines when a macro event occurs and which community pair fires. Placement information determines the post-import microstate. Both can affect prediction.

\begin{proposition}[Exact placement criterion]
\label{prop:placement}
Fix a microscopic state $x$ with $a^\star_{ij}(x)>0$ and condition on an $i\to j$ transmission event. The two-scale import rule reproduces the immediate post-event destination-state distribution of the full CTMC if and only if
\begin{equation}
  \Pi_{ij}(v\mid x)=\pi^\star_{ij}(v\mid x)\qquad \text{for all }v\in S_j(x),
\end{equation}
where $\pi^\star_{ij}$ is defined in Eq.~\eqref{eq:exact_target_distribution}.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Conditioning on an $i\to j$ event fixes the community-level event but not the target node. In the full CTMC, the target is distributed according to the edge-conditioned law $\pi^\star_{ij}$. In the two-scale model, it is distributed according to $\Pi_{ij}$. The post-event destination state is obtained by infecting the selected target. The post-event laws coincide exactly when the target distributions coincide.
\end{proof}

\subsection{A sufficient interface for exact cross-community coupling}

\begin{proposition}[Interface-sufficient exactness]
\label{prop:interface_sufficient}
Consider an SIR or SI process in which a cross-community infection event changes only the destination node from $S$ to $I$. Suppose the interface information at time $t$ contains, for every ordered pair $i\neq j$, the exact active intensity $a^\star_{ij}(X(t))$ and the exact target distribution $\pi^\star_{ij}(\cdot\mid X(t))$. If macro event times are generated from the total intensity $\sum_{i\neq j}a^\star_{ij}(X(t))$, if the firing pair is selected proportionally to $a^\star_{ij}$, and if the target is selected from $\pi^\star_{ij}$, then the two-scale construction reproduces the full-network CTMC law.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The local channels are identical to the full CTMC within each $V_i$. The proposed interface uses the exact total cross-community intensity, the exact distribution of the firing community pair, and the exact destination-target distribution conditional on that pair. Since a cross-community infection changes only the destination node state, the source identity is not needed to update the epidemic configuration. Therefore every possible next transition and its propensity matches the full generator in Eq.~\eqref{eq:full_generator}. Repeating the argument after each jump gives equality of CTMC laws.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The proposition does not say that the macro layer must know the entire microscopic graph. It identifies sufficient statistics for exact epidemic-state coupling: active pair intensities and target distributions. Practical interface-aware approximations can estimate these quantities from compact summaries.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Accumulated hazard and survival prediction}

Even a good instantaneous mean predictor does not automatically preserve first-arrival distributions when it is used as a plug-in deterministic hazard. Let
\begin{equation}
  A_t=\int_0^t a^\star_{ij}(X(s))\,ds
\end{equation}
be the full accumulated cross-community hazard for a single event channel or motif, and let $\bar A_t^{\calI}=\Eop[A_t\mid\calI_t]$ be the accumulated hazard predicted from incomplete information.

\begin{proposition}[Jensen gap for plug-in survival]
\label{prop:jensen}
For any information set $\calI_t$,
\begin{equation}
  \Eop\bigl[e^{-A_t}\mid\calI_t\bigr]
  \geq e^{-\Eop[A_t\mid\calI_t]}
  =e^{-\bar A_t^{\calI}}.
  \label{eq:jensen_survival}
\end{equation}
Consequently, replacing a random accumulated hazard by its conditional mean can underestimate survival and predict earlier first arrivals in the minimal one-channel setting.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The function $z\mapsto e^{-z}$ is convex. Jensen's inequality conditional on $\calI_t$ gives Eq.~\eqref{eq:jensen_survival}.
\end{proof}

