\begin{remark}
This is a mechanism, not a universal sign theorem for all two-scale simulations. Recovery, placement, repeated imports, and downstream state changes can compensate for or reverse the signed effect. The statement says that averaging hazard before applying the nonlinear survival transform changes the predicted first-arrival law unless the remaining hazard uncertainty is negligible.
\end{remark}

\section{Clique--bridge--leaf motif}
\label{sec:clique_leaf}

The clique--bridge--leaf motif is the minimal network in which incomplete interface information matters. Community $0$ is a clique $K_n$ with node set $V_0$. Community $1$ is a single susceptible leaf $A$. A bridge set $B\subset V_0$ of size $k$ connects to $A$, and no other node in the clique has an edge to the leaf. For analytical clarity, consider SI dynamics without recovery.

