The chain benchmark also clarifies the role of network structure. Dense cliques mix rapidly, so bridge infection may quickly become representative of community prevalence. Sparse communities, long paths to interface nodes, or heterogeneous gateway placement can make prevalence a weak predictor of export readiness. Thus, the relevant structural quantity is not prevalence alone but the relation between prevalence, internal mixing, and export-relevant contact geometry.

\section{Scope: why controlled benchmarks before real-world case studies?}
\label{sec:scope}

\michaelreplace{A real-world case study is an important next step, but it is not the cleanest first test of the theory. The present paper studies the value of network information. To measure that value, one needs to know both the full-information reference and the incomplete-information abstraction. In many empirical settings, the full contact network is precisely the unobserved object. Treating a partial dataset as if it were the full truth would mix three distinct effects: information loss caused by abstraction, measurement error in the observed network, and calibration error in epidemiological parameters.}

Controlled graph families avoid this ambiguity. In the clique--bridge--leaf motif, one can prove exactly which information is missing: the macro model sees prevalence but not bridge activation. In a chain of cliques, one can prove how local delay discrepancies can propagate. These motifs are not intended to replace empirical validation. They are designed to establish mechanisms that a later empirical study can test.

The next empirical step should therefore use public or privacy-preserving contact data with a meaningful community partition and compare three levels of information: a full-contact or high-resolution reference, a prevalence-only two-scale abstraction, and an interface-aware abstraction. The prediction targets should include first-arrival distributions, post-import placement effects, and outbreak completion probabilities, not only mean prevalence curves.

\section{Discussion and outlook}
\label{sec:discussion}

\michaelreplace{The main message is that two-scale epidemic models should be evaluated as prediction models under incomplete network information. A full contact network determines exact inter-community intensities and target distributions. A micro--macro abstraction sees only selected summaries. The difference between these information sets determines what the reduced model can and cannot predict.}

A prevalence-only hazard is not automatically inadequate. It is appropriate when prevalence is informative about active cross-community exposure. This can occur when interface nodes are numerous, well mixed with the community interior, and not topologically special. It is less reliable when inter-community transmission depends on narrow bridge sets, hidden gateway geometry, or local paths that make export readiness lag behind overall infection growth.

The value-of-information identity suggests a constructive research program. Instead of asking whether the macro layer should receive the entire microscopic graph, one can ask which additional summaries produce the largest reduction in prediction uncertainty. Candidate summaries include infected interface exposure, susceptible destination exposure, degree-weighted gateway counts, and local placement distributions. Such summaries preserve the two-scale separation while making the interface more faithful to the full contact process.

The theory also clarifies why validation should emphasize first-arrival distributions and post-import placement, not only prevalence curves. Two models can have similar mean prevalence but different inter-community timing. In downstream chains, small timing discrepancies may accumulate. In recovery models, the same issue can affect completion probability or outbreak probability, because delayed or misplaced imports may fade out differently.

Future work should develop three directions. First, empirical network case studies should estimate the predictive value of interface-aware summaries on real or high-quality synthetic contact networks. Second, statistical calibration should distinguish between fitting a scalar mobility rate and adding information to the interface; a scalar correction cannot recover distinctions between microscopic states that are identical at the macro level. Third, a separate computational-science simulation paper should study algorithmic efficiency, implementation choices, and runtime scaling. The present ANS paper is concerned with the information question: how prediction accuracy changes when the full contact network is replaced by a two-scale abstraction.

